  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n   We propose an improved strategy based on the observation that the original optimization struggles with the highly non-convex objective and local minima. The approach combines:\n   - Enhanced initialization using feasibility-preserving random starts\n   - Gradient-based optimization with constraint maintenance\n   - Automated projection to recover infeasible solutions\n   The key insight is that maintaining strict feasibility prevents invalid solutions from derailing progress, while diverse initializations improve global optimum chances.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   Define h: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] as a step function with:\n   - \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1 \u21d2 sum(h) = n_points / 2\n   - C\u2085 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1 - h(x+k)) dx\n   The problem requires min C\u2085 over feasible h. The objective is non-convex but locally smooth.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   a. Validate parent construction if available\n   b. Initialize with 50 random feasible points:\n      i. For each, generate uniform samples, scale to sum 0.5\n   c. Use SLSQP with:\n      - Initial points from 50 above\n      - Gradient-based steps\n      - Post-step projection to [0,1]^n_points\n   d. Track best solution via:\n      i. Continuous feasibility checks\n      ii. Value comparisons\n      iii. Recovery mechanisms\n   e. Terminate at budget or no improvement\n\n4. Search Choices\n   - Use 50 distinct initial guesses for broad coverage\n   - Apply adaptive gradient steps with conservative tolerance\n   - Limit evaluation budget to 1100s as specified\n\n5. Compute Budget\n   - Initializations: 50 \u21d2 50 * (60 steps) = 3000 evaluations\n   - SLSQP: 300 iterations per \u21d2 300 * 50 = 15,000\n   - Total under budget\n\n6. Validation\n   - Ensure h \u2208 [0,1]^n\n   - Check sum(h)=0.5\n   - Recompute C\u2085 as objective",
